.Dd November 26, 2026
.Dt GPU-CHECK.DB 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm gpu-check.db
.Nd the database of the runs of gpu-check
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
file is the record of the runs of
.Xr gpu-check 8 .
It is a SQLite database, and it holds one table for the runs, one for the
cards, one for the checks, one for the properties of the machine and one
for the events that are the source of truth of all of them.
.Pp
Every run of the tool writes to it.
The tool never reads it while a run lasts: the events are written as the
run goes, and the tables that hold the current state are written in the
same transaction as the event that caused them.
When a machine is pulled out in the middle of a run, the events that were
written are still there and the run is marked as not finished.
.Pp
The file is written in the write-ahead log mode, so a reader may read it
while a run is writing to it.
Each event is its own transaction, and a snapshot of the properties is one
transaction, so a snapshot is either whole or not there at all.
.Pp
The schema of the file is versioned.
The version is in the
.Pa schema_version
table, as a string and as its three parts, so a reader never has to parse
a version to compare it.
A file whose version is newer than the one the tool knows is refused, and
the refusal names both versions: the one of the file and the one of the
tool.
The migrations are in
.Pa /usr/local/share/gpu-check/schema ,
one file per version, and they are applied when the file is new and when
the file is older than they are.
.Sh TABLES
.Bl -tag -width "property_values" -compact
.It Pa schema_version
The version of the file: one row, always.
.It Pa migrations
The migrations that have been applied, in the order they were applied.
.It Pa platforms
The operating systems a run happened on.
The core of the tool names no platform of its own; it asks this table.
.It Pa sources
Where a fact came from: the sysctl tree, the kernel environment, the
loader, the module list, the device tree, the operating system.
.It Pa property_keys
The names of the properties, one per source.
.It Pa properties
What a property is: its kind, fixed the first time it is seen.
.It Pa property_classes
How a property behaves in one run: constant, changing or a counter.
The class is of the run and not of the key, so a key that is busy in one
run may be quiet in the next.
.It Pa property_values
The value history of a property: one row per value, closed when the value
changes.
A value that does not change does not make a row.
.It Pa observations
What a snapshot saw: the link between a snapshot and the value of a
property at that moment.
.It Pa snapshots
The state of the machine at a point of the run: one at the start of the
run, before the checks have run, and one at the end, after they have run.
The difference between the two is what the run changed.
.It Pa runs
The runs: when they started and ended, the tool and its version, the mode,
the architecture, the exit status, the host as a salted hash, the commit
of the test kit and the boot environment.
.It Pa devices
The cards of a run: their identity, the decision that was taken about them
and the reason for it.
.It Pa device_features , Pa device_arch_values , Pa device_warnings , Pa device_loaded
The lists of a card.
A list is a table of its own and never a column.
.It Pa check_defs , Pa check_deps
What each check is and what it needs.
A check keeps its id when its title changes.
.It Pa run_checks , Pa check_steps
What a check did in a run, and the steps it took.
.It Pa metric_defs
The metrics the tool knows.
A metric that is not defined here has no value: the tool refuses to invent
one.
.It Pa measurements
The value of a metric, at a moment, for a card.
.It Pa blobs
The pieces of a run that are too big, or too odd, to be a column.
A blob is never parsed for a result; it is insurance.
.It Pa events
The events.
Everything else is derived from these rows, and these rows are only ever
added to.
.It Pa event_data
What an event carried: one row per name and value, so the payload of an
event is a table and not a document.
.El
.Pp
The keys are integers and the natural key of a row is unique.
The foreign keys are declared and enforced, and the check constraints
hold what a column may hold.
There is no list in a column and there is no document in a column.
.Sh READING A RUN BACK
The
.Nm gpu-check
command reads the file back, and the answers it gives are the same facts
the tables hold:
.Bl -tag -width "compare" -compact
.It Cm gpu-check history
The runs that are in the file.
.It Cm gpu-check show Ar run
One run, as the document of the run.
.It Cm gpu-check compare Ar run Ar run
Two runs, side by side.
.It Cm gpu-check export Ar run
One run, as JSON.
.It Cm gpu-check properties Ar run
The properties of a run, and the ones that changed.
.El
.Pp
The events are the source of truth and everything else is derived from
them, so a reader may rebuild the tables from the events alone.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sqlite3 1 ,
.Xr gpu-check 8
.Sh CAVEATS
The file holds no personal data: the host is written as a salted hash and
the UUID of a card is hashed, never printed.
The file is not a secret, but it holds the state of the machine it was
written on.
